---
import AppShell from '../layouts/AppShell.astro';
// qa1018 C15 (skeptic SKP-03): /security said "No telemetry by default" while
// this page documented an on-by-default daily heartbeat — and neither page
// mentioned install events. Both pages now say the SAME thing, sourced from
// the code (loopskill-api):
//   * install events — app/models.py InstallEvent (skill, version, time,
//     client_ip, api_key_id, bundle_id) written by
//     app/_skill_helpers.py::_record_install_event; the ?ref=/utm_* tag rides
//     along for attribution. MCP-tool installs carry no request IP.
//   * heartbeat — app/heartbeat_client.py: opt-OUT via RECIPES_TELEMETRY.
//   * page views — POST /api/pv from blog + use-case pages only
//     (src/pages/blog/[slug].astro): path, utm_* tags, referrer.
// The one-paragraph summary is mirrored on /security ("Telemetry" card);
// tests/qa1018-w1-chrome.test.ts pins that both pages say it.
const lastUpdated = '2026-10-18';
---

<AppShell mode="public" title="Privacy — LoopSkill" description="What LoopSkill records (install events, an opt-out daily heartbeat, blog page views, account data), what it does not, and how to opt out.">
  <article class="prose max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 py-16 md:py-20" data-privacy>
    <p class="eyebrow mb-2">Trust &amp; legal</p>
    <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-semibold tracking-tight mb-3">Privacy</h1>
    <p><em>Last updated: {lastUpdated}</em></p>

    <h2>The 30-second version</h2>
    <ul data-telemetry-summary>
      <li><strong>When you install a skill</strong>, our server records an install event so we can count installs: which skill and version, the time, the <code>ref</code>/<code>utm</code> tag if your link had one, the requesting IP address, and your API key if you used one.</li>
      <li><strong>Installer clients send one anonymous heartbeat per day</strong> — a random salt and the date, nothing else. It is <strong>on unless you opt out</strong> with <code>RECIPES_TELEMETRY=off</code> (legacy variable name, still canonical).</li>
      <li><strong>Blog and use-case pages</strong> send a page-view count: the page path, any <code>utm</code> tags in the link, and the referring page.</li>
      <li><strong>Account data</strong> (email, Google or GitHub account ID, Stripe customer ID) is stored only when you create an account or subscribe.</li>
      <li>Skills run on your own machine. We do not see what they do there, or the files they work on.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Install events</h2>
    <p>
      An install through the API (for example <code>GET /api/skills/install</code>) writes one row: the skill
      and version, the time, the bundle it came from (if any), the <code>ref</code>/<code>utm</code>
      attribution tag (if any), the requesting IP address, and the API key that made the request (if any).
      We use these rows to count installs — the install numbers on this site come from them — and to filter
      out our own test traffic. Free skills install without a key; then no account is linked to the event.
    </p>

    <h2>What the heartbeat actually contains</h2>
    <p>Installer clients that use our heartbeat module send one HTTP request per day to <code>POST /api/v1/heartbeat</code>, unless you opt out (see below). The body has <strong>exactly two fields</strong>:</p>
    <pre><code>{`{
  "salt": "<32-character random hex string, generated on first run>",
  "last_seen_day": "YYYY-MM-DD"
}`}</code></pre>
    <p>Anything else in the request body is rejected with HTTP 400. The server enforces this with a strict Pydantic schema (<code>extra=&quot;forbid&quot;</code>).</p>

    <h2>What the heartbeat stores</h2>
    <p>The heartbeat table <code>fleet_pings</code> has <strong>only four columns</strong>:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>id</code> — synthetic primary key</li>
      <li><code>salt_hash</code> — <code>blake2b(salt, key=server_pepper)</code> — keyed hash, not the salt itself</li>
      <li><code>last_seen_day</code> — the day you sent the ping</li>
      <li><code>created_at</code> — server-side timestamp (used only for the 90-day TTL job)</li>
    </ul>
    <p>That table has <strong>no</strong> column for IP address, user-agent, OS, region, account ID, email or install path. It cannot answer &quot;who installed what&quot; — only &quot;how many distinct devices were active in week N&quot;.</p>

    <h2>Why the heartbeat is anonymous</h2>
    <p>Two layers:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Schema</strong>: the heartbeat table has no personal-data columns, so there is no per-person query to run.</li>
      <li><strong>Hashing</strong>: <code>blake2b</code> is keyed by a server-side pepper. Even with a full copy of the database, an attacker could not map a hash back to a salt without also stealing the pepper from a separate secrets store.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Retention</h2>
    <p>Heartbeat rows are deleted after 90 days by a weekly cron job. Aggregate weekly counts (e.g. &quot;week of 2026-W18 had N active devices&quot;) are kept indefinitely.</p>

    <h2>Opting out of the heartbeat</h2>
    <p>Set the environment variable on your machine before running any LoopSkill command:</p>
    <pre><code>export RECIPES_TELEMETRY=off</code></pre>
    <p>The variable still has its old name (from before the LoopSkill rename) — it is the one the code reads. Accepted values (case-insensitive): <code>off</code>, <code>0</code>, <code>false</code>, <code>no</code>, <code>disable</code>, <code>disabled</code>. With this set, the heartbeat client stops before any network call. We test this with mocked HTTP transports to assert <em>zero</em> outbound traffic.</p>

    <h2>Account data</h2>
    <p>If you create an account or subscribe via Stripe Checkout, we additionally store: your email, OAuth provider ID (GitHub or Google), Stripe customer ID, subscription tier, and (optionally) a Discord user ID if you link Discord for role-gated channels. None of this is shared with third parties beyond the providers themselves (Stripe for billing, Resend for transactional email, GitHub/Google for OAuth).</p>

    <h2>Questions</h2>
    <p>Email <a href="mailto:privacy@wisechef.ai">privacy@wisechef.ai</a>. Product questions: <a href="mailto:hello@loopskill.io">hello@loopskill.io</a>. See also the <a href="/terms">Terms</a> and <a href="/security">Security &amp; audit</a>.</p>
  </article>
</AppShell>
